<article class="env-lemma" id="0DZD">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0DZD">Lemma <span data-tag="0DZD">93.14.3</span>.</a> In Example <a href="/tag/0DY1" data-tag="0DY1">93.8.1</a> let $P$ be a $k$-algebra. Let $J \subset P$ be an ideal. Denote $(P^ h, J^ h)$ the henselization of the pair $(P, J)$. If </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$k \to P$ is of finite type, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(P) \to \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(k)$ is smooth on the complement of $V(J)$, 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> then the functor between deformation categories of Lemma <a href="/tag/0DYT" data-tag="0DYT">93.8.8</a> </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \mathcal{D}\! \mathit{ef}_ P \longrightarrow \mathcal{D}\! \mathit{ef}_{P^ h}  \]
</div>
  <p> is smooth and induces an isomorphism on tangent spaces. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      We know that $\mathcal{D}\! \mathit{ef}_ P$ and $\mathcal{D}\! \mathit{ef}_{P^ h}$ are deformation categories by Lemma <a href="/tag/0DY2" data-tag="0DY2">93.8.2</a>. Thus it suffices to check our functor identifies tangent spaces and a correspondence between liftability, see Formal Deformation Theory, Lemma <a href="/tag/0DYP" data-tag="0DYP">90.20.3</a>. The property on liftability is proven in Lemma <a href="/tag/0DZB" data-tag="0DZB">93.14.1</a> and the isomorphism on tangent spaces is the special case of Lemma <a href="/tag/0DZC" data-tag="0DZC">93.14.2</a> where $N = B$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>